course: dictionary
lesson: 11835
title: Blaring
level: primary-5
stars: 0
explain: Blaring describes a sound that is very loud and harsh, so harsh that it is unpleasant to hear. «The music next door was blaring, so I could not hear my own thoughts.» The clues are the loud music and the fact that it drowned out everything else.
explain: Blaring can be an adjective, describing a noise like «blaring horns», or a verb, as in «the alarm is blaring». The base word is blare, and its other forms are blares, blared and blaring.
explain: Blaring sounds are often warnings. «The fire alarm was blaring, so everyone walked calmly out of the building.» When you hear a blaring alarm, it is a signal to act, not to ignore.
fact: Blaring means making a very loud, harsh, unpleasant noise.
fact: Blare is the base word: blare, blares, blared, blaring.
fact: «Blaring» can describe music, horns, sirens or alarms.
fact: «Blaring» is a signal that something is wrong or urgent, such as a fire alarm.
fact: When an alarm is blaring, leave the building calmly and do not stop to collect your things.
exercise: «The alarm was blaring through the school.» What does blaring mean? | Bright and colourful | *Very loud and harsh | Very quiet and soft | Slowly fading away
exercise: «A fire engine raced past with its siren blaring.» What is the siren doing? | *Making a loud, harsh noise | Playing a soft tune | Sleeping quietly
exercise: «The fire alarm was blaring.» What should you do? | Cover your ears and sit still | *Leave the building calmly | Hide under your desk and wait | Ask your friends to find the source of the noise
test: «The radio ___ all morning.» | *blared | blare | blares | blaring
test: What is the opposite of blaring? | *Quiet | Blaring | Loud | Harsh
exam: «The ___ music was so loud that Tom covered his ears.» | *Blaring | Whispered | Quiet | Gentle
